@startuml
title Builder / Fluent Builder

skinparam legendBackgroundColor #f9f9f9
skinparam legendBorderColor #999999

legend right

**Intent**:
Separate construction from representation
so the same build process can create different representations.

**Motivation**:
Creating complex objects (e.g., HTTP requests, immutable DTOs)
with many optional parts, controlling construction sequence.

**Applicability**:
Complex construction logic; need to vary internal composition.
Want fluent, readable assembly; avoid telescoping constructors.

**Participants & Collaborations**:
Director orchestrates steps.
Builder defines steps and retrieval of result.
ConcreteBuilder provides specific part assembly.

**Consequences**:
Pros: Clear separation of steps; supports varying representations; readable fluent API.
Cons: More types and code; sometimes overkill for simple construction.

**Implementation Notes**:
Fluent methods returning this (mutable) or new instances (immutable).
For thread-safety, prefer immutable builders returning new objects.
Director is optional; client can drive steps directly.

**Known Uses**:
Java’s StringBuilder, Stream.Builder.
HTTP clients building requests (OkHttp, Spring WebClient, Apache HttpClient).

**Related Patterns**: Abstract Factory (families), Prototype (copying),
Composite (assembling nested parts).

endlegend


class Director {
  +construct(builder: Builder)
}

interface Builder {
  +buildPartA()
  +buildPartB()
  +getResult() : Product
}

class ConcreteBuilder1
class ConcreteBuilder2
class Product

Director ..> Builder : orchestrates
Builder <|.. ConcreteBuilder1
Builder <|.. ConcreteBuilder2
ConcreteBuilder1 ..> Product : builds
ConcreteBuilder2 ..> Product : builds
@enduml